INT. UNCLAIMED UMBRELLA DEPOT - TRANSIT LOST PROPERTY BASEMENT - DAY

Eleven thousand umbrellas hang from the ceiling pipes like a congregation of sleeping bats, each tagged, each waiting for an owner who stopped believing in them years ago. Fluorescent tubes hum a half-tone flat.

DELPHINE OKONKWO-REYES (50s, silver bob, a cardigan buttoned one hole too high) pours tea from a thermos into five paper cups with the care of a priestess. The cups match. Nothing else in the room does.

BARNABY QUILL (40s, rumpled, a man who irons only the collar) sits on an overturned crate, polishing a single brass umbrella handle that no one has asked him to polish.

TOMOKO ARAI (30s, sharp-eyed, hair pinned with a pencil) tallies index cards into stacks. The stacks are very straight.

GUS PRZYBYLSKI (60s, broad, a retired bus driver who took this job "for the quiet") winds a wall clock that has not told the right time since March.

MEERA SUNDARAM-HOLT (20s, the newest, lanyard still stiff) holds her cup with both hands and does not drink.

Delphine sets the thermos down. Squares it to the table edge. Smiles at nobody.

                    DELPHINE
          Lovely to have everyone down here at
          once. Truly. It's been, what, since the
          Christmas lunch?

                    BARNABY
          The lunch was in the stairwell.

                    DELPHINE
          Cozy, though.

                    TOMOKO
          Four hundred and twelve intake forms this
          month. Up nine percent.

                    DELPHINE
          Nine! Wonderful. People keep losing
          things. It's the one thing we can count
          on.

                    GUS
          Count on. Right.

                    DELPHINE
          Gus, you look well. Is that a new coat?

                    GUS
          Nineteen ninety-eight.

                    DELPHINE
          Timeless.

                    MEERA
          Is there an agenda?

                    DELPHINE
          Agenda. No, no. Just a catch-up. We never
          catch up.

                    BARNABY
          Delphine, the elevator has been shut off
          since eight this morning.

                    DELPHINE
          Maintenance.

                    BARNABY
          Nobody told maintenance, I asked.

                    DELPHINE
          Then it's a surprise maintenance.

                    TOMOKO
          I've never seen the upstairs door locked
          from the outside.

                    DELPHINE
          Draughts. The building has draughts.

                    GUS
          Nice tea.

                    DELPHINE
          Oolong. Someone brought it in. I think it
          was left behind by a customer and nobody
          claimed it, so, technically, ours.

                    MEERA
          That's in the policy. Ninety days, then
          disposal or reassignment.

                    DELPHINE
          Meera knows the policy. Meera is going to
          go far.

                    MEERA
          Is that a promotion?

                    DELPHINE
          It's an observation.

                    BARNABY
          What's in the folder?

                    DELPHINE
          Which folder?

                    BARNABY
          The one under the thermos. Cream. Five
          tabs. Five names.

                    DELPHINE
          Those are the, ah, the umbrella
          reconciliation sheets.

                    TOMOKO
          I do the reconciliation sheets. They're
          green.

                    DELPHINE
          New format.

                    GUS
          They've got our names on the tabs.

                    DELPHINE
          Because we're all, you know, reconciled.

                    BARNABY
          Delphine.

                    DELPHINE
          Barnaby.

                    BARNABY
          How long have you had the cups?

                    DELPHINE
          What?

                    BARNABY
          Five matching cups. This building has
          never owned five matching anything.

                    DELPHINE
          I wanted it to be nice.

                    TOMOKO
          For what?

                    DELPHINE
          For whatever it is.

                    GUS
          Ninety days, then disposal or
          reassignment.

                    MEERA
          I said that.

                    GUS
          I know you did, love.

Delphine slides the cream folder across the table. Her hand does not shake. Her thermos lid, left unscrewed, rolls in a slow half-circle and drops off the edge.

Nobody picks it up. Above them, one umbrella turns on its hook in air from no vent, and every tag along the pipe begins, softly, to tick.
